WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.037
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4517.

2
00:00:01.037 --> 00:00:02.074
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.074 --> 00:00:04.147
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alrighty today is September twenty-fourth, 2015.

4
00:00:04.147 --> 00:00:10.368
I'm Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:10.368 --> 00:00:15.897
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

6
00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:19.699
Today we're at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:19.699 --> 00:00:22.118
I'm here today with Mrs. Gloria Rogers.

8
00:00:22.118 --> 00:00:24.537
So glad you could make it today.

9
00:00:24.537 --> 00:00:26.956
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:26.956 --> 00:00:31.449
This is going to be a short interview about fifteen to twenty minutes.

11
00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:39.743
And we will likely be in touch to set up a later interview for a little bit longer if you're interested in continuing on.

12
00:00:39.743 --> 00:00:40.088
Alright.

13
00:00:40.088 --> 00:00:43.890
So for the record could you please state your full name?

14
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:44.926
<v GLORIA ROGERS>Gloria Kilgore Rogers.

15
00:00:44.926 --> 00:00:45.963
<v ARSENEAULT>Kilgore is K-I-L-G-O-R-E?

16
00:00:45.963 --> 00:00:46.309
<v ROGERS>R-E.

17
00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

18
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.105
<v ROGERS>And Rogers is R-O-G-E-R-S.

19
00:00:49.105 --> 00:00:49.632
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

20
00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:52.789
<v ROGERS>Some spell it with a D.

21
00:00:52.789 --> 00:00:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And when and where were you born?

22
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.778
<v ROGERS>I was born in Shreveport, Louisiana on April eighth, 1934.

23
00:01:04.778 --> 00:01:07.889
<v ARSENEAULT>And your parents' names?

24
00:01:07.889 --> 00:01:11.778
<v ROGERS>Cora Willis and Robert Kilgore.

25
00:01:11.778 --> 00:01:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And Willis is . . . Can you spell that?

26
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.227
<v ROGERS>W-I-L-L-I-S.

27
00:01:19.227 --> 00:01:24.136
<v ARSENEAULT>And your grandparents' names?

28
00:01:24.136 --> 00:01:25.364
<v ROGERS>Savannah.

29
00:01:25.364 --> 00:01:27.818
That's S-A-V-A-N-N-A-H.

30
00:01:27.818 --> 00:01:33.955
Savannah Willis and Richard Willis.

31
00:01:33.955 --> 00:01:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And that was your mom . . . Your dad's side?

32
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.269
<v ROGERS>That was my mother's parents.

33
00:01:48.269 --> 00:01:52.846
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember your father's parents' names?

34
00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:56.115
<v ROGERS>No. I never knew them.

35
00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:56.769
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

36
00:01:56.769 --> 00:02:02.000
So where did you live growing up?

37
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.770
<v ROGERS>I lived in Arcadia.

38
00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.848
That's A-R-C-A-D-I-A, Louisiana.

39
00:02:06.848 --> 00:02:11.697
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you describe your house growing up?

40
00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:20.008
<v ROGERS>The first house I lived in was the house of my parents.

41
00:02:20.008 --> 00:02:23.471
It was a wooden . . . Wooden.

42
00:02:23.471 --> 00:02:24.164
W-O-O-D-E-N.

43
00:02:24.164 --> 00:02:26.242
A wooden house.

44
00:02:26.242 --> 00:02:29.012
There were four rooms.

45
00:02:29.012 --> 00:02:37.324
And there were . . . The four . . . Of the four rooms two were bedrooms.

46
00:02:37.324 --> 00:02:40.094
One was a kitchen.

47
00:02:40.094 --> 00:02:43.557
What was the third one?

48
00:02:43.557 --> 00:02:53.254
Let's see, well it must have been three bedrooms and one was the kitchen.

49
00:02:53.254 --> 00:03:03.643
And we had an outhouse, and then as the time progressed we advanced a little.

50
00:03:03.643 --> 00:03:09.184
My mother passed in . . . On May first, 1950.

51
00:03:09.184 --> 00:03:24.422
Then my father at that time was living in Los Angeles, and my mother's wish was that we would always stay together.

52
00:03:24.422 --> 00:03:28.578
We being my brother and myself.

53
00:03:28.578 --> 00:03:35.504
And therefore my grandparents took us in and reared us.

54
00:03:35.504 --> 00:03:39.660
And I stayed . . . Well what grade?

55
00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:46.586
I was in the eleventh grade when my mother passed.

56
00:03:46.586 --> 00:03:53.512
So we moved in with . . . That was in 1950 . . . Yeah.

57
00:03:53.512 --> 00:04:05.980
We moved in with our grandparents, and then when I graduated from high school I went to college.

58
00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:12.906
My brother graduated . . . When he graduated from high school he

59
00:04:12.906 --> 00:04:20.525
<c.p>started college, but he didn't stay because he didn't like it.

60
00:04:20.525 --> 00:04:25.373
So he joined the United States Navy.

61
00:04:25.373 --> 00:04:31.607
And he stayed in the navy until he retired.

62
00:04:31.607 --> 00:04:42.689
And after he retired from the navy he got a job with the state correctional center.

63
00:04:42.689 --> 00:04:51.000
And he stayed there until he became ill and he passed.

64
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:57.136
After I . . . Well I graduated from high school before he did.

65
00:04:57.136 --> 00:05:02.715
After I graduated from high school I went to college.

66
00:05:02.715 --> 00:05:06.620
I stayed in college for four years.

67
00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:16.661
And after I graduated from college I landed my first job and that was in this area, Mossville.

68
00:05:16.661 --> 00:05:28.934
That was the first . . . In fact when I came here, Mossville High School was the only black high school in this area.

69
00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:33.397
So I worked there until I believe 1969.

70
00:05:33.397 --> 00:05:50.132
And then 1969 . . . Well prior to 1969 there were rumors that they were going to close the high school and some teachers would be transferred to Westlake High School, [Westlake,

71
00:05:50.132 --> 00:06:01.289
Louisiana] some would be transferred to Sulphur High School [Louisiana] and . . . Because they were going to maintain the elementary school,

72
00:06:01.289 --> 00:06:11.331
but the high school that was grade nine through twelve, the teachers in that area were being transferred.

73
00:06:11.331 --> 00:06:24.161
When we . . . And when I used to work we . . . There were about five of us who left Mossville and went to Westlake High.

74
00:06:24.161 --> 00:06:31.971
The other high school teachers like I said were transferred to Sulphur High School.

75
00:06:31.971 --> 00:06:38.665
When we went to Westlake High School the principal was really receptive.

76
00:06:38.665 --> 00:06:46.475
But some of the faculty members appeared as though they didn't want us there.

77
00:06:46.475 --> 00:06:54.843
It was not our desire that we go there, but because of the school system,

78
00:06:54.843 --> 00:07:06.000
and at that time integration had come in to being, and they were trying to meet the federal guidelines . . .

79
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.290
<v ARSENEAULT>Were y'all the first black teachers to teach there?

80
00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:15.533
<v ROGERS>No. There were two black teachers from when we went there.

81
00:07:15.533 --> 00:07:24.112
When we went there, I think they had been there maybe a year, maybe a couple of years.

82
00:07:24.112 --> 00:07:26.019
Well they had gotten

83
00:07:26.019 --> 00:07:29.355
<c.p>adjusted to them, so that was okay.

84
00:07:29.355 --> 00:07:38.888
But when we left Mossville High and went there, not all of the teachers . . . Some teachers were really, really receptive.

85
00:07:38.888 --> 00:07:40.794
The principal was receptive.

86
00:07:40.794 --> 00:07:46.037
And as I said, some of the teachers were really receptive.

87
00:07:46.037 --> 00:07:49.374
I was sandwiched between two English teachers.

88
00:07:49.374 --> 00:07:53.187
One to my left, one to my right.

89
00:07:53.187 --> 00:07:59.860
The teacher to my right . . . Oh she was so cooperative; she was so nice.

90
00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:08.439
She said, "Mrs. Rogers my name is . . ." I've forgotten the first name but the last name was Clark.

91
00:08:08.439 --> 00:08:13.206
And she said, "I teach eleventh and tenth grade English.

92
00:08:13.206 --> 00:08:19.402
If there's anything I can do to help you, please let me know."

93
00:08:19.402 --> 00:08:24.645
Okay, the other teacher to my left taught twelfth grade English.

94
00:08:24.645 --> 00:08:27.505
I believe she had all seniors.

95
00:08:27.505 --> 00:08:27.981
Okay.

96
00:08:27.981 --> 00:08:31.794
I had tenth, eleventh, and twelfth grade English.

97
00:08:31.794 --> 00:08:39.421
And those students who came to me, of course it was a mixture, they were cooperative.

98
00:08:39.421 --> 00:08:44.664
I remember one day I was . . . It was my planning period.

99
00:08:44.664 --> 00:08:50.383
So I said to myself, "I'm going to go to the library,"

100
00:08:50.383 --> 00:08:59.916
because I wanted to see when I would assign research papers what information the students would be able to get.

101
00:08:59.916 --> 00:09:07.065
And I went to that library and I was just browsing around and to me,

102
00:09:07.065 --> 00:09:17.075
of course might have just been me, it appeared as though the librarian thought I wanted something out of that library.

103
00:09:17.075 --> 00:09:28.991
So finally I said, "Ma'am I'm just checking to see what materials my students will be able to use once I assign the research paper."

104
00:09:28.991 --> 00:09:39.000
Because at that time the computer . . . It was in existence but not to the multiplicity that we have them now.

105
00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.674
<v ARSENEAULT>She just told me the food’s ready.

106
00:09:40.674 --> 00:09:44.022
Do you want to come back, or do you want to finish a little?

107
00:09:44.022 --> 00:09:44.978
It's up to you?

108
00:09:44.978 --> 00:09:45.217
<v ROGERS>Okay.

109
00:09:45.217 --> 00:09:46.891
<v ARSENEAULT>We can pause it and come back.

110
00:09:46.891 --> 00:09:47.130
<v ROGERS>Okay.

111
00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:47.609
Pause it.

112
00:09:47.609 --> 00:09:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay we'll pause it, and we can go eat.

113
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:50.357
<v ROGERS>Yeah.

114
00:09:50.357 --> 00:09:51.786
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah [break in tape].

115
00:09:51.786 --> 00:09:56.071
<v ROGERS>And then sometimes if you don't have the cooperation of the parents.

116
00:09:56.071 --> 00:09:57.500
You have a problem.

117
00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:57.857
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

118
00:09:57.857 --> 00:09:59.286
And that was it?

119
00:09:59.286 --> 00:10:03.571
A lot of the parents just didn't . . . They just didn't have a . . .

120
00:10:03.571 --> 00:10:10.000
<v ROGERS>No, because some parents blame the teacher for what their child is doing or is not doing.

121
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:10.346
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

122
00:10:10.346 --> 00:10:14.846
<v ROGERS>And if the truth were known it's because it starts in the home.

123
00:10:14.846 --> 00:10:15.192
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

124
00:10:15.192 --> 00:10:19.000
<v ROGERS>And you can’t train them when you come . . . I mean . . .

125
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:19.351
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

126
00:10:19.351 --> 00:10:20.053
I know.

127
00:10:20.053 --> 00:10:21.807
Well we can start there.

128
00:10:21.807 --> 00:10:23.561
Is that recording or no?

129
00:10:23.561 --> 00:10:24.614
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Yeah we’re recording.

130
00:10:24.614 --> 00:10:25.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh I'm sorry.

131
00:10:25.667 --> 00:10:28.123
Okay so we're back with Mrs. Rogers.

132
00:10:28.123 --> 00:10:31.982
We were . . . We left off talking about . . . I think you were . . .

133
00:10:31.982 --> 00:10:33.737
<v ROGERS>The situation at Westlake High.

134
00:10:33.737 --> 00:10:34.088
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

135
00:10:34.088 --> 00:10:34.439
Yes.

136
00:10:34.439 --> 00:10:39.000
Did you want to tell us a little bit more about that?

137
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:47.192
<v ROGERS>Well I think I was talking about the day I had gone into the library,

138
00:10:47.192 --> 00:10:54.838
and it seemed to me as though I was not wanted in the library.

139
00:10:54.838 --> 00:11:00.846
I can see mentally the librarian just standing up observing me.

140
00:11:00.846 --> 00:11:09.038
And I'm thinking to myself, “I'm certainly not going to take anything out of here.”

141
00:11:09.038 --> 00:11:21.600
But I had gone to see what materials our library had that the students would be able to use to do their research.

142
00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:30.338
Eventually after I had looked a while I left, and I went back to the room.

143
00:11:30.338 --> 00:11:34.708
And I was just so . . . I was upset,

144
00:11:34.708 --> 00:11:45.085
because not that the librarian said anything to me, but it was the way she was looking at me.

145
00:11:45.085 --> 00:11:48.362
And then I calm myself down.

146
00:11:48.362 --> 00:11:51.092
I said, "Oh my God.

147
00:11:51.092 --> 00:11:52.185
That's life.

148
00:11:52.185 --> 00:11:53.277
I'm black.

149
00:11:53.277 --> 00:11:54.369
She's white."

150
00:11:54.369 --> 00:12:00.923
And I don't mean to imply that all white people exemplify that

151
00:12:00.923 --> 00:12:08.023
<c.p>attitude sometimes by what they don't say, and how they just observe you.

152
00:12:08.023 --> 00:12:20.585
But that's . . . It's not that way with all of them because I think I said earlier, I was in between two English teachers.

153
00:12:20.585 --> 00:12:26.592
The one to my right taught tenth and eleventh grade English.

154
00:12:26.592 --> 00:12:30.962
The one to my left twelfth grade English.

155
00:12:30.962 --> 00:12:37.515
And of course the one to the right she was so nice.

156
00:12:37.515 --> 00:12:40.246
And I see her mentally.

157
00:12:40.246 --> 00:12:45.162
You know when you start reflecting on certain things?

158
00:12:45.162 --> 00:12:46.800
I see her.

159
00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.808
And I say to myself, "I wonder what happened to her?"

160
00:12:52.808 --> 00:13:01.000
I really don't know because I was only at Westlake High for three years.

161
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:08.904
At the time, it was during the summer, I was in Dayton, Ohio with my husband.

162
00:13:08.904 --> 00:13:14.337
And I got a telephone . . . I got a . . . What was that?

163
00:13:14.337 --> 00:13:15.819
An overnight letter.

164
00:13:15.819 --> 00:13:21.747
And the letter was stating: call Mr. [Russell] at the school board.

165
00:13:21.747 --> 00:13:29.651
And the number was indicated and about the time of the day that I should call.

166
00:13:29.651 --> 00:13:35.578
And I said to myself, "Why do I have to call him?"

167
00:13:35.578 --> 00:13:39.036
And I said, "I wonder what happened?"

168
00:13:39.036 --> 00:13:45.458
But the bottom line was, the first time I called he wasn't in.

169
00:13:45.458 --> 00:13:49.410
So I said, "Well, I'll wait for tomorrow."

170
00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.867
And I called and he was in.

171
00:13:52.867 --> 00:13:53.855
Excuse me.

172
00:13:53.855 --> 00:13:58.301
And I told the person who answered the phone.

173
00:13:58.301 --> 00:14:05.217
I said, "I had gotten this mail that I'm supposed to call Mr. Russell.”

174
00:14:05.217 --> 00:14:07.193
And I identified myself.

175
00:14:07.193 --> 00:14:11.639
He came to the phone and he said, "Hello."

176
00:14:11.639 --> 00:14:13.120
I said, "Hello."

177
00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.084
I said, "I am Gloria Rogers.

178
00:14:16.084 --> 00:14:21.518
I received this mail that I was supposed to call you."

179
00:14:21.518 --> 00:14:23.494
He said, "Oh yes.

180
00:14:23.494 --> 00:14:24.482
Oh yes."

181
00:14:24.482 --> 00:14:27.940
And I said, "You know I'm curious."

182
00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.410
He said, "I can imagine."

183
00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:40.289
So he said, "Mrs. Rogers we’re going to move you from Westlake High to Barbe High School [Lake Charles, Louisiana]."

184
00:14:40.289 --> 00:14:42.759
And I said, "To Barbe?"

185
00:14:42.759 --> 00:14:44.241
He said, "Yes."

186
00:14:44.241 --> 00:14:46.711
I said, "You know what?

187
00:14:46.711 --> 00:14:50.169
I like it at Westlake High now."

188
00:14:50.169 --> 00:14:54.614
I said, "When I first went there I didn't."

189
00:14:54.614 --> 00:15:04.000
I said, "Because the first two or three days that we were there I was a nervous wreck."

190
00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:11.252
And I explained to him why I was so nervous, because there were policemen on the hall.

191
00:15:11.252 --> 00:15:17.650
Students were . . . When it would be time for the students to change the police would

192
00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:27.462
<c.p>come out, and they would make sure that the students would not be milling around the hall but instead go to their classes.

193
00:15:27.462 --> 00:15:30.874
And I said, "At first it was something."

194
00:15:30.874 --> 00:15:34.713
I said, "But I'm beginning to like it now."

195
00:15:34.713 --> 00:15:38.979
He said, "Well we're assigning you to Barbe High School."

196
00:15:38.979 --> 00:15:44.951
And I said, "Sir, is there any other school that I could go to?"

197
00:15:44.951 --> 00:15:50.497
He said, "I believe we have enough teachers for the other high schools."

198
00:15:50.497 --> 00:15:52.629
I said, "Oh my goodness."

199
00:15:52.629 --> 00:15:56.469
He said, "But you would like it at Barbe."

200
00:15:56.469 --> 00:16:05.000
I said, "I don't have any choice if I can't go to another school because I need to work."

201
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
So he asked me when was I coming back to Lake Charles and I told him.

202
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
He said, "When you get to Lake Charles call the school board office and let them know when you will be able to meet me at Barbe High School.

203
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
And he said, "I would arrange it so that I'll be able to meet you there then.

204
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
Because you know where Barbe High School is?"

205
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
I said, "Not really."

206
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
I said, "I have an idea.”

207
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
So he said . . . And he was trying to describe to me where it was but I'm saying to myself,

208
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
"Oh I'll find out before I go."

209
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
Anyway, it ended up we met at Barbe.

210
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
He introduced me to the principal.

211
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
At that time the principal was John Nicosia.

212
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
He seemed like a really nice person.

213
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
And we talked and he gave me a schedule of the classes that I would be teaching.

214
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
And he showed me where my room would be.

215
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
So he said, "You think you going to like it here?"

216
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
I said, "Sir, I really don't know."

217
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
But the bottom line is this: I began to like it.

218
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
The students were . . . They were really nice.

219
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
They were really nice.

220
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
My being there the first couple of days that the students came, and mentally I'm reflecting on the first day I went to Westlake High.

221
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
What a difference there was in the attitudes of the students.

222
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
And I need to say this, not all the students at Westlake High were reluctant to have a black teacher.

223
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
Not all of them.

224
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.200
Some of them were really nice.

225
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:09.720
And back to Barbe.

226
00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:28.602
My first year there I believe I was assigned to teach tenth and eleventh grade English.

227
00:16:28.602 --> 00:16:42.764
And I'm laughing because when some of the students came into the

228
00:16:42.764 --> 00:16:49.845
<c.p>room they were saying different things.

229
00:16:49.845 --> 00:16:59.286
I heard them I just didn't say anything.

230
00:16:59.286 --> 00:17:02.826
I just listened.

231
00:17:02.826 --> 00:17:22.888
So after they got settled and everything I told them who I was and blah, blah, blah.

232
00:17:22.888 --> 00:17:34.689
And one of the students said, "You know, Mrs. Rogers?"

233
00:17:34.689 --> 00:17:42.950
I said, "Oh you remember my name."

234
00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:45.311
"Yes ma'am.

235
00:17:45.311 --> 00:17:57.112
You know we were at Welsh High School [Welsh, Louisiana]."

236
00:17:57.112 --> 00:18:00.652
I said, "Really?"

237
00:18:00.652 --> 00:18:07.733
"And we ran the teacher off."

238
00:18:07.733 --> 00:18:18.354
I said, "What are you trying to tell me?

239
00:18:18.354 --> 00:18:25.435
You planning to run me off?"

240
00:18:25.435 --> 00:18:31.335
I said, "I don't believe."

241
00:18:31.335 --> 00:18:47.857
I said, "Now if I leave it's not because you will run me off."

242
00:18:47.857 --> 00:19:06.739
I said, "Because I plan to be here as long as God allows me to live."

243
00:19:06.739 --> 00:19:23.773
So they got blah blah blah . . . Then when I started telling them the classroom things they were supposed to do and shouldn’t do and blah, blah, blah.

244
00:19:23.773 --> 00:19:25.444
They got so quiet.

245
00:19:25.444 --> 00:19:28.368
I said to myself, "I'm hitting home."

246
00:19:28.368 --> 00:19:37.141
And then they start challenging me on the rules that I had given and they said, "Well suppose I do this?

247
00:19:37.141 --> 00:19:38.812
Suppose I do that?"

248
00:19:38.812 --> 00:19:40.901
I said, "Look, look, look.

249
00:19:40.901 --> 00:19:42.572
We’re terminating that discussion.

250
00:19:42.572 --> 00:19:45.914
I've already told you what my expectations are.

251
00:19:45.914 --> 00:19:49.674
Now, I want to know what your expectations are."

252
00:19:49.674 --> 00:19:53.016
And some of them were really, really rational.

253
00:19:53.016 --> 00:19:57.193
But some of them were just, you know, playing around.

254
00:19:57.193 --> 00:19:59.700
So I said, "Okay young man.

255
00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:00.535
Come on.

256
00:20:00.535 --> 00:20:02.624
We going to stop that."

257
00:20:02.624 --> 00:20:04.713
I said, "You know what?

258
00:20:04.713 --> 00:20:14.739
You here for a purpose and so am I." I said, "I'm here to teach you and I hope you are here to learn."

259
00:20:14.739 --> 00:20:16.828
They got very, very quiet.

260
00:20:16.828 --> 00:20:23.512
And I explained to them what the class was like, or what it would be like.

261
00:20:23.512 --> 00:20:28.107
And we discussed . . . I just let them talk the first day.

262
00:20:28.107 --> 00:20:38.551
I'm feeling my way to try to get some indication as to where I would have to grow to be able to meet their needs.

263
00:20:38.551 --> 00:20:46.906
And as the time progressed the students began to be so receptive and one thing I told them . . . Excuse me.

264
00:20:46.906 --> 00:20:49.830
"You can't be late for my class."

265
00:20:49.830 --> 00:20:54.008
"And if I'm late what are you going to do?"

266
00:20:54.008 --> 00:20:56.514
I said, "I tell you what.

267
00:20:56.514 --> 00:20:57.350
Find out."

268
00:20:57.350 --> 00:20:59.021
But you know what?

269
00:20:59.021 --> 00:21:07.794
When that tardy bell would ring if they were not in the room in their seats, I would close the door.

270
00:21:07.794 --> 00:21:11.554
And they would come and knock on the door.

271
00:21:11.554 --> 00:21:14.478
And I would go to the door.

272
00:21:14.478 --> 00:21:17.402
I said, "Do you have your excuse?"

273
00:21:17.402 --> 00:21:18.238
"No ma'am."

274
00:21:18.238 --> 00:21:18.655
"Sorry

275
00:21:18.655 --> 00:21:20.744
<c.p>you can't come in here."

276
00:21:20.744 --> 00:21:24.504
And they wanted to know, "Where am I going?"

277
00:21:24.504 --> 00:21:28.264
I said, "You need to go to the principal.

278
00:21:28.264 --> 00:21:30.352
But I tell you what.

279
00:21:30.352 --> 00:21:37.037
If you're not back within a certain length of time I'm going to call the office."

280
00:21:37.037 --> 00:21:41.214
So I never had any problem with that child anymore,

281
00:21:41.214 --> 00:21:48.316
because he was determined . . . I mean he was able to see that I wasn't tolerating that foolishness.

282
00:21:48.316 --> 00:21:52.076
And he became one of my very best students.

283
00:21:52.076 --> 00:21:55.000
No, do you want to pause?

284
00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:55.380
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

285
00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.280
We can pause right here.

286
00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:01.080
<v ROGERS>Because you’re not interested in what went on at Barbe.

287
00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:04.120
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh definitely . . . We're interested in all of it.

288
00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:06.780
But we're just under a time constraint.

289
00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:07.920
So if you’re . . .

290
00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:14.000
<v ROGERS>Yeah, because I . . . I mean my thinking was that you were primarily concerned about Mossville.

291
00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:15.061
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah well . . . We'll get . . .

292
00:22:15.061 --> 00:22:16.388
<v ROGERS>Since this is the area.

293
00:22:16.388 --> 00:22:16.653
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

294
00:22:16.653 --> 00:22:21.429
We want mostly Mossville, but also kind of like for context just all of your history as well.

295
00:22:21.429 --> 00:22:27.000
So we have questions about just you, and then questions about Mossville so we can kind of get an idea.

296
00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:27.463
<v ROGERS>Yeah.

297
00:22:27.463 --> 00:22:35.335
You know that was one thing I was reflecting as we were sitting at the table eating.

298
00:22:35.335 --> 00:22:39.966
There was one thing I really should have told you.

299
00:22:39.966 --> 00:22:45.059
When I first came here, it was in August of 1955.

300
00:22:45.059 --> 00:22:47.374
Well I told you that.

301
00:22:47.374 --> 00:22:55.709
But one question I had in my mind, “Why is it that they're busing students from Vinton Louisiana,

302
00:22:55.709 --> 00:23:01.266
Sulphur Louisiana, into this school right here in the Old Spanish Trail?”

303
00:23:01.266 --> 00:23:08.675
And of course at that time I know that there were not . . . Schools were not integrated.

304
00:23:08.675 --> 00:23:19.788
But I couldn't understand why these black kids in Vinton had to be bused to this school here, because their parents were paying taxes.

305
00:23:19.788 --> 00:23:27.197
Sulphur High School . . . The black kids in Sulphur had to be bused to this school here.

306
00:23:27.197 --> 00:23:33.680
In other words, they're leaving their environment in order to come to this school.

307
00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:37.384
But that's just the way the system was.

308
00:23:37.384 --> 00:23:41.089
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah we want to get all of that.

309
00:23:41.089 --> 00:23:41.552
<v ROGERS>Okay.

310
00:23:41.552 --> 00:23:46.645
<v ARSENEAULT>So could we schedule a follow up with you for later?

311
00:23:46.645 --> 00:23:49.424
Maybe for some time next week?

312
00:23:49.424 --> 00:23:53.128
Yeah, we can go ahead and pause it.

313
00:23:53.128 --> 00:23:57.296
Just thank you so much for your time and . . .

314
00:23:57.296 --> 00:23:57.759
<v ROGERS>Okay.

315
00:23:57.759 --> 00:24:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>For sharing what you did today.

316
00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:01.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4517.

317
00:24:01.429 --> 00:24:01.857
End Session I.]
